#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""await-approval: wait in the background for an out-of-band approval, then exit.

    await-approval <id> [--kind NAME] [--timeout SEC]

Some actions are approved by a person clicking a link, away from the agent's
thread: a remote session, a write grant, a deploy gate. The click does not wake
the thread, so a typed "Approved" used to be the only wake signal. This script
long-polls a STATUS tool on an MCP server (through bin/mcp-http-call.py) until the
request leaves PENDING, prints ONE line with the final state, and exits. Run it
with the Bash tool's run_in_background: its exit is a background-task
notification, which wakes an idle thread with no typed reply. It is the FALLBACK
for a host that has no callback URL to hand the approval server; where the
server can call a webhook on approve, deny or expire, no waiter is needed.

Nothing here names a server. The server, tool and arguments of each kind of
request are configuration (below), with a neutral built-in kind, so a copy that
has no approval server configured simply reports that and exits 2.

Every exit code the waiter can return:

    0      approved: the request is OPEN (and, where the kind says so, usable by this caller)
    3      not approved, and it will not become so (the line names the state and the reason):
             state=denied      the approver pressed Deny (status CLOSED with reason denied,
                               or a bare DENIED, DECLINED or REJECTED status)
             state=expired     the pending window ran out (status EXPIRED, or reason pending_expired)
             state=closed      closed for another reason (idle, disconnect, error, revoked, max, closed)
             state=not_usable  the request is OPEN but this caller did not request it (the
                               kind's usable_field is false), so acting on it would be
                               refused; never exit 0
    124    timeout: --timeout passed with the request still PENDING (state=timeout)
    2      error: bad usage or configuration, no caller, no API key, the server refused the
           key, the id is unknown to the server, an answer this script cannot read, or the
           server kept failing
    128+N  stopped by signal N before the server answered (state=interrupted): 143 SIGTERM
           (the host stopping a background task), 130 SIGINT, 129 SIGHUP; nothing was decided

    await-approval: session 3f2a91bc state=open exit=0 waited=312s idle_left=900s
    await-approval: session 3f2a91bc state=expired exit=3 waited=1800s reason=pending_expired
    await-approval: session 3f2a91bc state=denied exit=3 waited=95s reason=denied
    await-approval: grant 8c1d44e0 state=open exit=0 waited=41s usable=yes
    await-approval: grant 8c1d44e0 state=not_usable exit=3 waited=41s usable=no reason=requested_by_another_caller
    await-approval: session 3f2a91bc state=error exit=2 waited=1s detail="not_found: No such session..."

HOW to run it from an agent:
  - request every approval the task needs first, post the approve links as the last
    lines of the turn, start this with run_in_background, end the turn. One waiter per id.
  - pass an explicit Bash timeout of at least 1900000 ms: the default --timeout is 1860 s
    (a 1800 s pending window plus margin), longer than the 30-minute unattended background
    limit some hosts apply.
  - on exit 3, read the state. denied: the approver said no, so say so and stop. expired or
    closed: post nothing and wait for the operator, because re-requesting for an approver who
    is away loops. not_usable: the request belongs to another caller, so request one from this
    session (or check that the waiter's key is this session's key). On exit 124 re-run the
    waiter only if the server says the request is still PENDING. On exit 128+N the waiter was
    stopped and nothing was decided: re-run it if the request is still PENDING. On exit 2
    report the line. After exit 0, make the real call the approval was for.
  - the id may itself be the credential for the follow-up call, so the line prints only its
    first 8 characters, never an approve URL, and never a key.

THE STATUS CONTRACT the tool must answer (a flat JSON object, or the same object under the
kind's "record" key), whatever the server:
  status                     PENDING, OPEN, CLOSED or EXPIRED (any case)
  close_reason / closed_reason  why it closed: "denied", "pending_expired", or anything else
  pending_seconds_remaining, idle_seconds_remaining   optional, shown in the line
  <usable_field>             optional boolean on the answer (not the record), for a kind that
                             sets usable_field: whether THIS caller may use an OPEN request
An answer with an "error" key is an error (exit 2). The tool should hold the call until the
request changes state or about wait_s seconds pass, so the call returns the moment the
approver clicks; a tool that answers PENDING at once is paced, never hammered.

CONFIGURATION. A JSON file, $AWAIT_APPROVAL_CONFIG or ~/.config/ops/await-approval.json
(an explicit AWAIT_APPROVAL_CONFIG that does not exist is an error; the default file may be
absent). Without one the built-in kind "default" applies: tool approval_status, arguments
{"id": <id>, "wait_s": <seconds>}, a flat record, on the endpoint OPS_MCP_URL names.

    {
      "default_kind": "session",
      "key_connectors": ["approvals"],
      "kinds": {
        "session": {"server": "approvals", "tool": "session_status",
                    "args": {"session_id": "{id}", "wait_s": "{wait}"}},
        "grant":   {"server": "approvals", "tool": "grant",
                    "args": {"mode": "status", "grant_id": "{id}", "wait_s": "{wait}"},
                    "record": "grant", "usable_field": "usable_by_this_caller"}
      }
    }

  server        endpoint name for mcp-http-call.py --server (needs OPS_MCP_URL_TEMPLATE), or
                null to use OPS_MCP_URL
  tool          the status tool's name (a suffix, as mcp-http-call.py resolves it)
  args          the tool's arguments; the string "{id}" becomes the id and "{wait}" the wait in
                whole seconds (an integer); every other value is passed as written
  record        the key of the status record inside the answer, or null for the answer itself
  usable_field  the answer key that says whether this caller may use an OPEN request, or null
  key_connectors  names of mcpServers entries in ~/.claude.json whose literal "Bearer <key>"
                header is the last-resort key (see below); default none

HOW it polls: one run of mcp-http-call.py per poll (`[--server S] call TOOL args out`), wait_s
at most 40 (the usual cap on one held call), shorter on the last poll before the deadline. A
poll that returns early with PENDING is paced to at least 3 s. Each poll opens one MCP session
and closes it with an HTTP DELETE, so at most one session per waiter is ever live. SIGTERM,
SIGINT and SIGHUP are passed to the poll in flight so its session still closes.

A transient failure (a 5xx or 429, a dropped connection, a tool answer with isError) is retried
up to 5 times in a row with a growing pause; a refused key (HTTP 401 or 403), an unknown
endpoint (404), an unknown tool, a missing configuration or any in-band "error" answer stops at
once. The HTTP status and the other fatal markers are read where mcp-http-call.py puts them, in
the first "ERROR: " line it prints, never in the response body quoted after it: a 502 whose
body says "HTTP 403" is still a 502.

WHAT a box needs:
  1. bin/mcp-http-call.py beside this script, and python3 (standard library only).
  2. The MCP endpoint: OPS_MCP_URL, or OPS_MCP_URL_TEMPLATE plus a kind's "server" name.
  3. The API key, read the way mcp-http-call.py reads it: OPS_MCP_API_KEY in the environment
     (or the variable OPS_MCP_KEY_VAR names), else that line of $OPS_DIR/.env (default
     ~/OPS/.env). When the poll reports no key, this script tries the literal "Bearer <key>"
     header of the key_connectors entries in ~/.claude.json and gives the key to the poll
     through the poll's environment only (never argv, never a file, never printed). A
     ${VAR} reference in the entry is not a key and is skipped.
  4. Outbound HTTPS to the endpoint. Nothing listens; no inbound URL is needed.
  The key must be the same person's key the agent session uses where the server binds a
  request to its requester: usable=no means the waiter's key is not the requester's.

Test-only environment (tests/await-approval-test.py): AWAIT_APPROVAL_MCP_CALL names a stub
for mcp-http-call.py; AWAIT_APPROVAL_PACE_S replaces the 3 s pacing and the retry pause
base; none changes behavior when unset.
"""
import argparse
import json
import os
import re
import shutil
import signal
import subprocess
import sys
import tempfile
import threading
import time

PROG = "await-approval"
EXIT_OK = 0
EXIT_ERROR = 2
EXIT_NOT_APPROVED = 3
EXIT_TIMEOUT = 124

DEFAULT_TIMEOUT_S = 1860  # a 1800 s pending window plus margin
MAX_TIMEOUT_S = 7200  # the longest a background command may run
MAX_WAIT_S = 40  # the usual cap on one held status call
MAX_FAILS = 5  # consecutive transient failures before the waiter gives up
CHILD_GRACE_S = 20  # a poll may run this long past its wait before it is cut off
ID_RE = re.compile(r"[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9_.:-]{3,127}")
KIND_RE = re.compile(r"[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9_-]{0,31}")
SERVER_RE = re.compile(r"[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]{0,40}")

# The kind a copy gets when it configures nothing: a neutral shape, no server name.
DEFAULT_CONFIG = {
    "default_kind": "default",
    "key_connectors": [],
    "kinds": {
        "default": {"server": None, "tool": "approval_status", "args": {"id": "{id}", "wait_s": "{wait}"},
                    "record": None, "usable_field": None},
    },
}

# The caller prints "ERROR: <message>" and the message may carry up to 800 characters of server
# text (a response body, a tool answer) that quotes anything at all. So every marker below is
# anchored to the start of the first "ERROR: " line (see error_line); a marker found later in the
# text is the server talking, not the client.
NO_KEY_RE = re.compile(r"ERROR: no API key")
# Failures a retry cannot fix.
FATAL = (
    (re.compile(r"ERROR: HTTP (401|403) on "),
     "the server refused the key (HTTP %s): check the API key (a stale value in the session "
     "environment shadows a rotated .env)"),
    (re.compile(r"ERROR: HTTP (404) on "), "endpoint not found (HTTP %s)"),
    (re.compile(r"ERROR: (?:no tool matching|ambiguous tool suffix)"), "the endpoint has no such tool"),
    (re.compile(r"ERROR: (?:no endpoint configured|--server needs|bad --server|the endpoint URL)"),
     "mcp-http-call.py refused the endpoint configuration"),
)
REASON_DENIED = "denied"  # close_reason or closed_reason of a Deny click
# A bare status that also means a denial, read as one if a server emits it.
DENIED_STATUSES = frozenset({"DENIED", "DECLINED", "REJECTED"})
# The states that exit 3: the request will never open for this caller.
NOT_APPROVED_STATES = ("expired", "closed", "denied", "not_usable")
# Where the caller finds the key besides the environment: $OPS_DIR/.env and ~/OPS/.env. Read
# here only to seed the scrub list; the poll reads its own key.
MIN_SCRUB_LEN = 8  # a shorter value is a placeholder; replacing it would garble the output

STOP = threading.Event()
_stop_signal = [0]
_child = [None]
_secrets = []


def key_var():
    """The name of the variable that holds the API key (mcp-http-call.py reads the same)."""
    return os.environ.get("OPS_MCP_KEY_VAR") or "OPS_MCP_API_KEY"


def env_files():
    files = []
    for base in (os.environ.get("OPS_DIR"), "~/OPS"):
        if base:
            path = os.path.expanduser(os.path.join(base, ".env"))
            if path not in files:
                files.append(path)
    return files


def _on_signal(signum, frame):
    """Remember the signal and pass it to the poll in flight, so that poll's session closes."""
    _stop_signal[0] = signum
    STOP.set()
    child = _child[0]
    if child is not None and child.poll() is None:
        try:
            child.terminate()
        except OSError:
            pass


def scrub(text, limit=240, ident=""):
    """One line, with every key value and any bearer token removed, cut to `limit`."""
    text = str(text)
    for secret in _secrets + [ident]:
        if secret:
            text = text.replace(secret, "<redacted>")
    text = re.sub(r"(?i)bearer\s+[A-Za-z0-9._~+/=-]{8,}", "Bearer <redacted>", text)
    # An approval URL can carry a different credential from the request id.
    # No server-supplied URL belongs in the waiter's result notification.
    text = re.sub(r"https?://[^\s<>\"']+", "<redacted-url>", text, flags=re.I)
    text = " ".join(text.split()).replace('"', "'")
    return text if len(text) <= limit else text[: limit - 3] + "..."


def short(ident):
    return ident[:8]


def finish(code, state, kind, ident, started, **extra):
    """Print the one result line and return the exit code."""
    parts = [PROG + ":", kind, short(ident), "state=%s" % state, "exit=%d" % code,
             "waited=%ds" % round(time.monotonic() - started)]
    detail = extra.pop("detail", None)
    for key, value in extra.items():
        if value not in (None, ""):
            value = scrub(value, ident=ident)
            if not re.fullmatch(r"[A-Za-z0-9_.:-]+", value):
                value = '"%s"' % value
            parts.append("%s=%s" % (key, value))
    if detail:
        parts.append('detail="%s"' % scrub(detail, ident=ident))
    print(" ".join(parts))
    sys.stdout.flush()
    return code


def usage_error(message):
    print("%s: %s" % (PROG, message), file=sys.stderr)
    print("usage: %s <id> [--kind NAME] [--timeout SEC]" % PROG, file=sys.stderr)
    return EXIT_ERROR


def pace_s():
    """Minimum seconds between polls, and the base of the retry pause."""
    try:
        return max(0.0, float(os.environ.get("AWAIT_APPROVAL_PACE_S", "3")))
    except ValueError:
        return 3.0


class ConfigError(ValueError):
    """The configuration file cannot be used; the message says where and why."""


def config_path():
    explicit = os.environ.get("AWAIT_APPROVAL_CONFIG")
    if explicit:
        return explicit, True
    return os.path.expanduser("~/.config/ops/await-approval.json"), False


def check_kind(name, kind, where):
    if not KIND_RE.fullmatch(name):
        raise ConfigError("%s: kind name %r is not letters, digits, '_' or '-'" % (where, name))
    if not isinstance(kind, dict):
        raise ConfigError("%s: kind %s is not an object" % (where, name))
    tool = kind.get("tool")
    if not isinstance(tool, str) or not re.fullmatch(r"[A-Za-z0-9_.:-]{1,100}", tool):
        raise ConfigError("%s: kind %s needs a \"tool\" name" % (where, name))
    server = kind.get("server")
    if server is not None and (not isinstance(server, str) or not SERVER_RE.fullmatch(server)):
        raise ConfigError("%s: kind %s has a \"server\" that is not an endpoint name or null" % (where, name))
    args = kind.get("args", {})
    if not isinstance(args, dict) or not all(isinstance(k, str) for k in args):
        raise ConfigError("%s: kind %s \"args\" is not an object" % (where, name))
    for field in ("record", "usable_field"):
        value = kind.get(field)
        if value is not None and (not isinstance(value, str) or not value):
            raise ConfigError("%s: kind %s \"%s\" is not a key name or null" % (where, name, field))
    return {"server": server, "tool": tool, "args": args, "record": kind.get("record"),
            "usable_field": kind.get("usable_field")}


def load_config():
    """The configuration: the file named by AWAIT_APPROVAL_CONFIG or the default path, else the
    built-in default. An explicit path that is missing, and any file that cannot be read or is
    not a valid configuration, raise ConfigError: a guess about what the operator meant could
    poll the wrong server."""
    path, explicit = config_path()
    if not os.path.exists(path):
        if explicit:
            raise ConfigError("AWAIT_APPROVAL_CONFIG names %s, which does not exist" % path)
        return DEFAULT_CONFIG
    try:
        with open(path) as fh:
            raw = json.load(fh)
    except (OSError, ValueError) as exc:
        raise ConfigError("cannot read %s: %s" % (path, exc))
    if not isinstance(raw, dict) or not isinstance(raw.get("kinds"), dict) or not raw["kinds"]:
        raise ConfigError("%s needs a \"kinds\" object with at least one kind" % path)
    kinds = {name: check_kind(name, kind, path) for name, kind in raw["kinds"].items()}
    default = raw.get("default_kind") or sorted(kinds)[0]
    if default not in kinds:
        raise ConfigError("%s: default_kind %r is not one of its kinds" % (path, default))
    connectors = raw.get("key_connectors", [])
    if not isinstance(connectors, list) or not all(isinstance(c, str) for c in connectors):
        raise ConfigError("%s: key_connectors is not a list of names" % path)
    return {"default_kind": default, "key_connectors": connectors, "kinds": kinds}


def find_caller():
    override = os.environ.get("AWAIT_APPROVAL_MCP_CALL")
    if override:
        return override if os.path.isfile(override) else None
    here = os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__))
    cand = os.path.join(here, "mcp-http-call.py")
    return cand if os.path.isfile(cand) else None


def claude_json_key(connectors):
    """A literal API key from a user-scope mcpServers entry of ~/.claude.json, or None.

    The last resort for a box whose key is in no environment and no .env. Only the entries
    the configuration names count, and only a literal "Bearer <key>"; a ${VAR} reference is
    not a key.
    """
    if not connectors:
        return None
    try:
        with open(os.path.expanduser("~/.claude.json")) as fh:
            servers = json.load(fh).get("mcpServers") or {}
    except (OSError, ValueError, AttributeError):
        return None
    if not isinstance(servers, dict):
        return None
    for name in connectors:
        entry = servers.get(name)
        headers = entry.get("headers") if isinstance(entry, dict) else None
        auth = headers.get("Authorization") if isinstance(headers, dict) else None
        if not isinstance(auth, str):
            continue
        match = re.fullmatch(r"Bearer\s+([A-Za-z0-9._~+/=-]{16,})", auth.strip())
        if match:
            return match.group(1)
    return None


def dotenv_keys():
    """Every value of the key variable in $OPS_DIR/.env and ~/OPS/.env, for scrub() only.

    mcp-http-call.py reads the first such line itself; this reads the files again so the
    waiter can redact the value, and prints and passes it nowhere. A missing or unreadable
    file is not an error here (the poll reports its own).
    """
    pattern = re.compile(r"(?:export\s+)?%s=(.*)" % re.escape(key_var()))
    keys = []
    for path in env_files():
        try:
            with open(path) as fh:
                lines = fh.read().splitlines()
        except (OSError, ValueError):
            continue
        for line in lines:
            match = pattern.fullmatch(line.strip())
            if match:
                value = match.group(1).strip().strip('"').strip("'")
                if len(value) >= MIN_SCRUB_LEN:
                    keys.append(value)
    return keys


class Poll:
    """The outcome of one run of the caller: a payload, or why it failed."""

    def __init__(self, payload=None, err="", fatal=False, transient=True, no_key=False):
        self.payload = payload
        self.err = err
        self.fatal = fatal
        self.transient = transient
        self.no_key = no_key

    @property
    def failed(self):
        return self.payload is None


def error_line(text):
    """The first "ERROR: " line of the poll's stderr, or "" when it printed none.

    That line is the caller's own verdict; what follows it may be server text.
    """
    for line in (text or "").splitlines():
        if line.startswith("ERROR: "):
            return line
    return ""


def classify_failure(stderr):
    text = " ".join((stderr or "").split())
    head = error_line(stderr)
    if NO_KEY_RE.match(head):
        return Poll(err=text, fatal=True, no_key=True)
    for pattern, message in FATAL:
        match = pattern.match(head)
        if match:
            groups = match.groups()
            return Poll(err=message % groups if "%s" in message else message, fatal=True)
    # Anything else is a gateway blip, a dropped connection or a tool answer with isError: retry,
    # and the poll limit ends it.
    return Poll(err=text or "no output from the caller")


def fill_args(template, ident, wait):
    """The kind's args with "{id}" and "{wait}" replaced (the wait stays an integer)."""
    out = {}
    for key, value in template.items():
        if value == "{id}":
            value = ident
        elif value == "{wait}":
            value = wait
        out[key] = value
    return out


def run_poll(runner, kind, spec, ident, wait_s, tmp, env, deadline):
    """One long poll through the caller; the answer's JSON lands in tmp/out.json."""
    args_path = os.path.join(tmp, "args.json")
    out_path = os.path.join(tmp, "out.json")
    try:
        os.unlink(out_path)
    except OSError:
        pass
    fd = os.open(args_path, os.O_WRONLY | os.O_CREAT | os.O_TRUNC, 0o600)
    with os.fdopen(fd, "w") as fh:
        json.dump(fill_args(spec["args"], ident, wait_s), fh)
    cmd = list(runner)
    if spec["server"]:
        cmd += ["--server", spec["server"]]
    cmd += ["call", spec["tool"], args_path, out_path]
    budget = max(5.0, min(wait_s + CHILD_GRACE_S, deadline - time.monotonic() + 5))
    try:
        proc = subprocess.Popen(cmd, stdin=subprocess.DEVNULL, stdout=subprocess.PIPE,
                                stderr=subprocess.PIPE, env=env, universal_newlines=True,
                                errors="replace")
    except OSError as exc:
        return Poll(err="cannot run the caller: %s" % exc.strerror, fatal=True)
    _child[0] = proc
    if STOP.is_set():  # a signal landed between the spawn and the hand-off above
        proc.terminate()
    try:
        try:
            _out, err = proc.communicate(timeout=budget)
        except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
            # Terminate, not kill: SIGTERM lets the poll send its session DELETE.
            proc.terminate()
            try:
                _out, err = proc.communicate(timeout=10)
            except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
                proc.kill()
                _out, err = proc.communicate()
            return Poll(err="poll timed out after %ds" % budget)
    finally:
        _child[0] = None
    if proc.returncode != 0:
        return classify_failure(err)
    try:
        with open(out_path) as fh:
            payload = json.load(fh)
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return Poll(err="the caller wrote no readable answer")
    if not isinstance(payload, dict):
        return Poll(err="the answer was not a JSON object")
    return Poll(payload=payload)


def interpret(spec, payload):
    """Map a status answer to (state, info): state is pending, open, expired, closed,
    denied, not_usable or error, and info carries the fields the result line prints."""
    record_key = spec["record"]
    outer = payload
    if record_key is None and "status" not in payload and isinstance(payload.get("result"), dict):
        payload = payload["result"]  # some servers wrap the answer in {"result": ...}
    elif record_key is not None and record_key not in payload and isinstance(payload.get("result"), dict):
        payload = payload["result"]
    # Unwrapping must not discard a refusal on the envelope. A status record
    # can still say OPEN when the status lookup itself failed for this caller.
    for answer in (outer, payload):
        if answer.get("error"):
            code = answer["error"] if isinstance(answer["error"], str) else answer.get("code", "error")
            message = answer.get("message") or ""
            return "error", {"detail": "%s: %s" % (code, message) if message else str(code)}
        if answer.get("success") is False:
            return "error", {"detail": "the status answer reports success=false"}
    usable = None
    if record_key is not None:
        record = payload.get(record_key)
        if not isinstance(record, dict):
            return "error", {"detail": "the answer carries no %s record" % record_key}
    else:
        record = payload
    if spec["usable_field"]:
        usable = payload.get(spec["usable_field"])
    status = str(record.get("status") or "").upper()
    reason = record.get("close_reason") or record.get("closed_reason")
    if status == "PENDING":
        left = record.get("pending_seconds_remaining")
        return "pending", {"pending_left": "%ds" % left if isinstance(left, (int, float)) else None}
    if status == "OPEN":
        if not spec["usable_field"]:
            left = record.get("idle_seconds_remaining")
            return "open", {"idle_left": "%ds" % left if isinstance(left, (int, float)) else None}
        # A request can be bound to the caller that made it: OPEN for someone else opens
        # nothing here (acting on it is refused), so exit 0 would be a lie.
        if usable is False:
            return "not_usable", {"usable": "no", "reason": "requested_by_another_caller"}
        if usable is not True:
            return "error", {"detail": "the answer does not say whether this caller can use the "
                                       "request (%s is missing)" % spec["usable_field"]}
        return "open", {"usable": "yes"}
    # The Deny button closes a PENDING request: status CLOSED, reason "denied".
    if reason == REASON_DENIED or status in DENIED_STATUSES:
        return "denied", {"reason": reason}
    if status == "EXPIRED" or reason == "pending_expired":
        return "expired", {"reason": reason}
    if status == "CLOSED":
        return "closed", {"reason": reason}
    return "error", {"detail": "unrecognised status %r" % status[:40]}


def wait_for_approval(args, config):
    kind, ident = args.kind, args.id
    spec = config["kinds"][kind]
    started = time.monotonic()
    deadline = started + args.timeout
    runner_path = find_caller()
    if runner_path is None:
        return finish(EXIT_ERROR, "error", kind, ident, started,
                      detail="mcp-http-call.py not found beside %s (a box needs bin/mcp-http-call.py "
                             "and python3)" % PROG)
    runner = [runner_path] if os.access(runner_path, os.X_OK) else [sys.executable, runner_path]
    env = dict(os.environ)
    var = key_var()
    if env.get(var):
        _secrets.append(env[var])
    # The poll may read its key from a file instead; know that value too so scrub() can
    # redact it from any text that reaches us unscrubbed (a traceback, a proxy page).
    _secrets.extend(dotenv_keys())
    tmp = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="await-approval-")  # mode 0700
    fails = retries = 0
    key_fallback_tried = False
    last = "pending"
    pending_left = None
    try:
        while True:
            if STOP.is_set():
                return finish(128 + _stop_signal[0], "interrupted", kind, ident, started)
            remaining = deadline - time.monotonic()
            if remaining <= 0:
                return finish(EXIT_TIMEOUT, "timeout", kind, ident, started, last=last,
                              pending_left=pending_left, retries=retries or None)
            # The last poll before the deadline asks for whatever whole seconds are left (0
            # is an immediate read), so the timeout reports a fresh state, not a stale one.
            wait_s = int(min(MAX_WAIT_S, remaining))
            began = time.monotonic()
            poll = run_poll(runner, kind, spec, ident, wait_s, tmp, env, deadline)
            if STOP.is_set():
                return finish(128 + _stop_signal[0], "interrupted", kind, ident, started)
            if poll.failed:
                if poll.no_key and not key_fallback_tried:
                    key_fallback_tried = True
                    key = claude_json_key(config["key_connectors"])
                    if key:
                        env[var] = key
                        _secrets.append(key)
                        continue
                if poll.no_key:
                    return finish(EXIT_ERROR, "error", kind, ident, started, retries=retries or None,
                                  detail="no API key: export %s, or put it in $OPS_DIR/.env (a box can "
                                         "also name a connector in key_connectors whose literal Bearer "
                                         "entry in ~/.claude.json holds it)" % var)
                fails += 1
                if poll.fatal or fails >= MAX_FAILS:
                    return finish(EXIT_ERROR, "error", kind, ident, started, retries=retries or None,
                                  detail=poll.err)
                retries += 1
                pause = min(30.0, max(pace_s(), 0.01) * (2 ** (fails - 1)))
                STOP.wait(min(pause, max(0.0, deadline - time.monotonic())))
                continue
            fails = 0
            state, info = interpret(spec, poll.payload)
            if state == "open":
                return finish(EXIT_OK, "open", kind, ident, started, retries=retries or None, **info)
            if state in NOT_APPROVED_STATES:
                return finish(EXIT_NOT_APPROVED, state, kind, ident, started,
                              retries=retries or None, **info)
            if state == "error":
                return finish(EXIT_ERROR, "error", kind, ident, started, retries=retries or None, **info)
            last = "pending"
            pending_left = info.get("pending_left")
            # A server that answers PENDING at once (a wait it ignored) must not be hammered.
            early = pace_s() - (time.monotonic() - began)
            if early > 0:
                STOP.wait(min(early, max(0.0, deadline - time.monotonic())))
    finally:
        shutil.rmtree(tmp, ignore_errors=True)


class Parser(argparse.ArgumentParser):
    def error(self, message):
        raise ValueError(message)


def parse(argv, config=DEFAULT_CONFIG):
    parser = Parser(prog=PROG, add_help=False)
    parser.add_argument("id", nargs="?")
    parser.add_argument("--kind", default=None)
    parser.add_argument("--timeout", default=str(DEFAULT_TIMEOUT_S))
    parser.add_argument("-h", "--help", action="store_true")
    args = parser.parse_args(argv)
    if args.help:
        return args
    if not args.id:
        raise ValueError("an id is required (the id the approval server returned for the request)")
    if args.kind is None:
        args.kind = config["default_kind"]
    if args.kind not in config["kinds"]:
        raise ValueError("--kind is one of: %s" % ", ".join(sorted(config["kinds"])))
    if not ID_RE.fullmatch(args.id):
        raise ValueError("the id must be the one the approval server returned")
    try:
        args.timeout = float(args.timeout)
    except ValueError:
        raise ValueError("--timeout is a number of seconds")
    if not 0 < args.timeout <= MAX_TIMEOUT_S:
        raise ValueError("--timeout is more than 0 and at most %d seconds" % MAX_TIMEOUT_S)
    return args


def main(argv):
    if "-h" in argv or "--help" in argv:  # the help works even when the configuration does not
        print(__doc__)
        return EXIT_OK
    try:
        config = load_config()
    except ConfigError as exc:
        return usage_error(exc)
    try:
        args = parse(argv, config)
    except ValueError as exc:
        return usage_error(exc)
    for sig in (signal.SIGTERM, signal.SIGINT, signal.SIGHUP):
        signal.signal(sig, _on_signal)
    return wait_for_approval(args, config)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
